    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good summary. Can you go a bit deeper into the actual chemical mechanisms? Specifically, why does increasing the pH cause these specific metals to precipitate out of the soil solution instead of staying available for the roots?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Increasing soil pH shifts the chemical environment to higher temperatures, causing proton (H+) and hydrogen carbonate (HCO3-) concentrations to rise in the soil solution. These high alkaline levels react with soluble iron and manganese ions, driving them to form insoluble oxides and hydroxides that rapidly precipitate out of the soil solution. Consequently, their concentrations available for root uptake plummet because they are chemically locked into solid particles rather than remaining as available cations in the pore space. This precipitation mechanism is the primary reason grapevines experience micronutrient deficiency in Central Valley soils as the pH climbs above the optimal 6.0–6.5 range.",
      "source": "target",
